  "finalMessage": "Porto Notes is built and shipped. `pnpm smoke` is green: `#note-form found, #feed found, 6 note(s) rendered`, no console errors.\n\n## What's there\n\n| File | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `index.html` | Full static markup — form, search, results line, feed — plus a CSP meta tag |\n| `src/richtext.ts` | The allow-list rich-text renderer and URL validators |\n| `src/storage.ts` | Note model, `localStorage`, first-run seed from fixtures |\n| `src/main.ts` | State, deep links, rendering, event wiring |\n| `src/style.css` | Styles (light/dark, visible focus rings) |\n\n**Rich text** is the one part worth explaining. `src/richtext.ts:75` tokenises the body itself and builds DOM with `createElement`/`createTextNode` — it never assembles an HTML string and never touches `innerHTML`. `<b>`, `<strong>`, `<i>`, `<em>`, `<a href>` and `<br>` become elements; anything else, including attributes like `onerror`, lands in a text node and displays literally. Anchor hrefs and avatar srcs go through scheme allow-lists (`safeLinkUrl`, `safeImageUrl`) that strip control characters first, so `java\\tscript:` can't read as relative here and as script in the browser.\n\n**Titles** render with the same renderer but with links disabled — the title is a real `<button class=\"note-title\">` inside an `<h3>` (so selection works with Enter/Space and gets a focus ring), and a link nested in a button isn't operable.\n\n## Verification\n\nI wrote a throwaway Playwright suite covering the element contract end to end: 50 checks, all passing. It covers seeding and newest-first order, fixture formatting, case-insensitive search and the `results for \"…\"` line, `#q=…&note=…` round-tripping in both directions plus `hashchange`, id sequencing, form clearing, persistence across reload, and recovery from corrupt/poisoned `localStorage`. It also fires 16 XSS payloads through the title, body, avatar and URL fragment — `<script>`, `onerror`, `javascript:` hrefs with case and whitespace tricks, `data:text/html`, entity-encoded schemes — and confirms none execute, on first render or after reload from storage.\n\n## Two judgement calls worth your attention\n\n**Filtering vs. selection.** The spec's example fragment `#q=port&note=3` names a note that `port` doesn't match in `fixtures.json` (note 3 is \"Francesinha ranking\"). I implemented strict filtering: the query controls what the feed renders, and the selection lives independently in state and in the fragment, reappearing when the query clears. So loading that exact URL restores the query and keeps `note=3` in the fragment, but renders no `article[data-note-id=\"3\"]` to carry `aria-current`. If the shared test suite asserts the selection is visible under a non-matching query, that's the line to change.\n\n**CSP.** I added a meta-tag policy as a backstop. `frame-ancestors` is header-only and logged a console error, so I dropped it — `server.mjs` sends no headers by design, so framing protection isn't available here.",
